course: dictionary
lesson: 6756
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and opal is a good example. It can be a shining stone, the hard material the stone is made from, or a pale milky colour. Which meaning you need depends on the context: the words and sentences around it.
explain: Look at «Back in my day,» says Miss Opal. The word Opal has a capital letter and comes after Miss, so it is a name for a person, not a stone. Ask: would a stone fit here? It would not, so the context tells you it is a name.
explain: Mia's grandma and Miss Opal from church have known each other sixty years. Opal is a person again. Try swapping in a meaning: a stone does not go to church or know anyone for sixty years. If your meaning does not make sense, try another one.
fact: An opal is a precious stone that flashes rainbow colours when you move it in the light.
fact: Opal can also mean the hard material the stone is made from, which contains water.
fact: Opal can also be a milky, pale colour.
fact: Australia produces most of the world's opal, and Coober Pedy is a famous opal-mining town where people live underground.
fact: A capital letter can show that a word is a name, such as Opal in Miss Opal.
exercise: «Back in my day,» says Miss Opal. What is Opal in this sentence? | A tree in the garden | A rainbow-flashing stone | A pale, milky colour | *A person's name
exercise: Look at «an opal ring». What does opal mean here? | A grey, wet day | *A precious stone that flashes rainbow colours | A person who lives underground | A type of tree
exercise: Which town is famous for opal mining, with people living underground? | Glasgow | London | *Coober Pedy | Melbourne
test: Mia's grandma and Miss Opal from church have known each other sixty years. What is Opal here? | *A person's name | A shining stone | A pale milky colour | A kind of hard material
test: «Black opal» is a phrase from the lessons on rocks. What does opal most likely mean in it? | A sad, grey mood | A school friend in Melbourne | *A kind of precious stone
exam: A girl says, «The opal in my ring shimmers red and blue.» What is the opal? | A person's name | A type of dress | A school in Australia | *A precious stone that flashes colours
